hinking</span></p></div></div><div data-node-id="1408:4556" data-name="Sub Heading" class="content-stretch flex items-center justify-center relative shrink-0 w-full"><p data-node-id="1408:4557" class="[word-break:break-word] flex-[1_0_0] font-['Roobert'] leading-[1.2] min-w-px not-italic relative text-[22.412px] text-[color:#2b2b2b]">Designing a Feedback Loop — Building Systems Around the Self</p></div></div><div data-node-id="1408:4558" data-name="Body" class="content-stretch flex items-center justify-center relative shrink-0 w-full"><div data-node-id="1408:4559" class="[text-box-edge:cap_alphabetic] [text-box-trim:trim-both] [word-break:break-word] flex-[1_0_0] font-['Plus_Jakarta_Sans'] font-normal leading-[0] min-w-px relative text-[12.607px] text-[color:#2b2b2b] whitespace-pre-wrap"><p class="leading-[1.4] mb-0">As a product designer, I saw patterns where others saw symptoms. My data wasn't just feedback—it became interface. With GPT, I analysed tone, sentiment, and pacing across daily journals to uncover cognitive and emotional drift. I was building a real-time mood engine—trained not on users, but on me. </p><p class="leading-[1.4]">Design became the structure for awareness. This wasn’t about diagnosis; it was about systematising understanding. GPT became the bridge between chaos and clarity—insight delivered through interface.</p></div></div></div><div data-node-id="1408:4560" class="absolute contents left-[calc(50%-10.86px)] top-[117.74px]"><div data-node-id="1408:4561" data-name="Flo-1" class="absolute bg-black h-[585.616px] left-[calc(50%-10.86px)] overflow-clip top-[117.74px] w-[683.556px]"><div data-node-id="1408:4562" class="absolute contents left-[70.03px] top-[59.29px]"><div data-node-id="1408:4563" class="absolute bg-gradient-to-b from-[rgba(119,21,245,0.2)] h-[141.397px] left-[70.03px] rounded-[35.853px] to-[#a966ff] top-[312.41px] w-[36.212px]"></div><div data-node-id="1408:4564" class="absolute bg-gradient-to-b from-[rgba(119,21,245,0.2)] h-[179.