ta-node-id="I1408:4533;4139:28100" data-name="HTL — Logo" class="h-[9.805px] relative shrink-0 w-[18.231px]"><img alt="" src="/assets/parity/1351-2283-56a9c.svg" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="absolute block inset-0 max-w-none size-full"></div><div data-node-id="I1408:4533;4139:28101" class="flex h-[12.607px] items-center justify-center relative shrink-0 w-0"><div class="flex-none rotate-90"><div data-name="Line" class="h-0 relative w-[12.607px]"><div class="absolute inset-[-0.7px_0_0_0]"><img alt="" src="/assets/parity/1351-2283-e939c.svg" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="block max-w-none size-full"></div></div></div></div><div data-node-id="I1408:4533;4139:28102" data-name="Text" class="content-stretch flex gap-[12.607px] items-center relative shrink-0"><div data-node-id="I1408:4533;4139:28103" data-name="Tilte" class="content-stretch flex items-center justify-center relative shrink-0"><p data-node-id="I1408:4533;4139:28104" class="[text-box-edge:cap_alphabetic] [text-box-trim:trim-both] [word-break:break-word] font-['Plus_Jakarta_Sans'] font-bold font-bold leading-[1.4] relative shrink-0 text-[12.61px] text-[color:black] whitespace-nowrap">Intrinsic</p></div><div data-node-id="I1408:4533;4139:28105" data-name="Sub Title" class="content-stretch flex items-center justify-center relative shrink-0"><p data-node-id="I1408:4533;4139:28106" class="[text-box-edge:cap_alphabetic] [text-box-trim:trim-both] [word-break:break-word] font-['Plus_Jakarta_Sans'] font-normal leading-[0] not-italic relative shrink-0 text-[0px] text-[color:black] whitespace-nowrap"><span class="font-['Roobert'] font-bold leading-[1.4] text-[18px]">Chapter 4 </span><span class="font-['Roobert'] leading-[1.4] text-[18px] text-black">— Always Thinking / Personal Endeavors (1983–Present) </span></p></div></div></div></div></div><div data-node-id="1408:4534" data-name="Textblock" class="absolute content-stretch flex flex-col gap-[33.618px] items-start left-[78.44px] top-[13.74px] w-[280.146px]"><div data-node-id="1408:4535" data-name="Headings" class="content-stretch flex flex-col gap-[22.412px] items-start relative shrink-0 w-full"><div data-node-id="1408:4536" data-name="Title" class="content-stretch flex flex-col items-start relative shrink-0 w-full"><div data-node-id="1408:4537" data-name="Heading" class="content-stretch flex items-center justify-center relative shrink-0 w-full"><p data-node-id="1408:4538" class="[text-box-edge:cap_alphabetic] [text-box-trim:trim-both] [word-break:break-word] flex-[1_0_0] font-['Roobert'] font-bold leading-[0] min-w-px not-italic relative text-[0px] text-[color:#2b2b2b]"><span class="font-['Plus_Jakarta_Sans'] font-light leading-none text-[33.618px]">4.4 — </span><span class="leading-none text-[33.618px]">Actigraphy and Circadian Rhythm Modelling</span></p></div></div><div data-node-id="1408:4539" data-name="Sub Heading" class="content-stretch flex items-center justify-center relative shrink-0 w-full"><p data-node-id="1408:4540" class="[word-break:break-word] flex-[1_0_0] font-['Roobert'] leading-[1.2] min-w-px not-italic relative text-[22.412px] text-[color:#2b2b2b]">Tracking Sleep to Track Stability — Using Wearables as Mood Predictors</p></div></div><div data-node-id="1408:4541" data-name="Body" class="content-stretch flex items-center justify-center relative shrink-0 w-full"><p data-node-id="1408:4542" class="[text-box-edge:cap_alphabetic] [text-box-trim:trim-both] [word-break:break-word] flex-[1_0_0] font-['Plus_Jakarta_Sans'] font-normal leading-[1.4] min-w-px relative text-[12.607px] text-[color:#2b2b2b]">I brought in wearables—Garmin, Strava—to passively capture sleep, heart rate variability, and daily rhythm. Over time, patterns emerged. Bipolar shifts weren’t random—they followed circadian drift. Actigraphy gave me the missing layer: foresight. Paired with AI, I began building visual timelines that aligned physical, cognitive, and emotional states. This allowed for predictive logic—seeing destabilisation before it struck. Where medication had failed to prevent episodes, data offered clarity.</p></div></div><div data-node-id="1408:4543" class="[word-break:break-word] absolute contents leading-[0] left-[calc(66.67%-10.74px)] text-[color:#2b2b2b] top-[-12.87px]"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Actigraphy" data-node-id="1408:4544" target="_blank" class="absolute block cursor-pointer font-['Big_Shoulders_Display'] font-bold font-black h-[20.696px] left-[calc(66.67%+27.78px)] text-[41.915px] top-[-12.87px] uppercase w-[272.45px]" rel="noopener noreferrer"><p class="leading-[35.628px] mb-0">​</p><p class="leading-[35.628px]">​</p></a><div data-node-id="1408:4545" class="absolute font-['Helvetica_Neue'] h-[21.011px] left-[calc(66.67%-10.74px)] not-italic text-[0px] top-[11.64px] w-[465.743px]"><p class="mb-0 text-[5.603px]"><span class="leading-[1.2]">Actigraphy is a non-invasive method of monitoring human rest/activity cycles. A small actigraph unit, also called an actimetry sensor, is worn for a week or more to measure gross motor activity. The unit is usually in a wristwatch-like package worn on the wrist. </span><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Actigraphy" target="_blank" class="[text-decoration-skip-ink:none] [text-underline-position:from-font] [word-break:break-word] cursor-pointer decoration-from-font decoration-solid font-['Helvetica_Neue'] leading-[1.2] not-italic text-[#2b2b2b] underline" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span class="[text-decoration-skip-ink:none] [text-underline-position:from-font] decoration-from-font decoration-solid underline">Wikipedia</span></a></p><p class="leading-[1.2] text-[5.603px]">​</p></div><p data-node-id="1408:4546" class="absolute font-['Gilroy'] left-[calc(66.67%-6.54px)] not-italic text-[11.171px] top-[360.42px] w-[477.649px]"><span class="leading-[12.575px]">Understanding the intricate relationship between sleep patterns and mood fluctuations is crucial in the realm of mental health. As a researcher or healthcare professional, utilizing actigraphy data becomes instrumental in unraveling the complex dynamics of these interconnected aspects. Actigraphy, a non-invasive method of monitoring sleep-wake cycles, provides a wealth of information that extends beyond merely tracking sleep duration. In this context, my motivation to leverage actigraphy data stems from a profound desire to gain deep insights into mood patterns. This innovative approach is made possible through the groundbreaking contributions of </span><span class="leading-[12.575px]">SSUPERLATIVE®</span><span class="leading-[12.575px]">, a pioneering company that is the brainchild behind the integration of actigraphy into mental health research and patient care. By employing this technology, we aim to proactively assist and support patients, potentially intervening early or even preventing episodes altogether. This integration of actigraphy into mental health research and patient care, driven by </span><span class="leading-[12.575px]">SSUPERLATIVE</span><span class="leading-[12.575px]">’s visionary ideas, represents a promising avenue for enhancing our understanding and, ultimately, improving the overall well-being of individuals facing mood-related challenges.</span></p></div><p data-node-id="1408:4547" class="[word-break:break-word] absolute font-['Big_Shoulders_Display'] font-bold font-black h-[284.348px] leading-[47.625px] left-[calc(66.67%-6.54px)] text-[56.029px] text-[color:black] top-[49.46px] uppercase w-[467.143px]">UNDERSTANDING intricate relationship between sleep patterns and mood fluctuations </p><div data-node-id="1408:4548" data-name="55D134D0-1A16-43C2-A1E3-F3F3C9051EBB_1_102_o 1" class="absolute h-[513.367px] left-[calc(25%+45px)] top-[17.24px] w-[481.591px]"><img alt="1.46" src="/assets/parity/1351-2283-b9375.png" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="absolute inset-0 max-w-none object-bottom pointer-events-none size-full"></div></div></SourceComposition>
